WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:03.899
23 years ago, Atlanta native,

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:05.489
an architecture and urban planning

00:00:05.490 --> 00:00:07.349
student, Ryan Gravel, had an

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:09.359
experience that opened his mind

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:11.249
to what urban living could be.

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:13.379
My senior year, I spent abroad

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:15.269
in Paris and lived

00:00:15.270 --> 00:00:17.219
without a car for a year, traveled

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:18.629
by train everywhere, and within a

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:20.609
month of arriving, I had lost 15

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:21.869
pounds. I was in the best shape of

00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:23.219
my life because I was walking

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:24.359
everywhere.

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:26.219
Paris fundamentally changed the way

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:27.809
that I understood the construction

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:29.759
of the world around me, and

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:31.019
I got obsessed with the role of

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:33.089
infrastructure as the foundation

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:35.429
for our economy, it's the foundation

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:37.079
for our social life and for our

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:38.069
culture.

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:39.839
For his master's thesis, Gravel

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:41.609
sketched out a plan to make Atlanta

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:43.109
more like Paris.

00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:44.999
He proposed redeveloping the land

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:46.949
along the city's historic rail lines

00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:48.899
to create a 22-mile loop called

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:50.609
the Atlanta Beltline.

00:00:50.610 --> 00:00:51.959
He proposed turning the city's

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:53.399
abandoned industrial areas

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:55.289
and single-family neighborhoods into

00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:56.939
business districts and walking

00:00:56.940 --> 00:00:58.019
trails.

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:00.119
And he proposed connecting downtown

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:01.949
to the rest of the city, all

00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:03.899
with a new train running

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:06.329
along the entire Atlanta Beltline.

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:07.559
Never imagined we would actually do

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:09.479
it, but just wanted to graduate.

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:12.329
But they did for the most part.

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:14.159
Cathy Woolard, who was president of

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:16.139
the Atlanta City Council, read

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:17.969
Gravel's thesis and decided

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:20.129
to use it as a blueprint to remake

00:01:20.130 --> 00:01:21.479
much of the city.

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:23.279
Today, the Atlanta Beltline is a

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:25.289
walking and biking trail, parts

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:27.209
of which are bordered by retail and

00:01:27.210 --> 00:01:28.079
condos.

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:29.579
But one piece of Gravel's grand

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:31.529
vision didn't get built.

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:32.519
The Train.

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:34.319
Today, gravel runs, co-working, and

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:35.969
event space along the Beltline,

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:37.589
which also serves as a gathering

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:39.599
place for urbanists interested in

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:41.309
making Atlanta less dependent on

00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:42.299
cars.

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:44.309
He says that the train line is

00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:47.189
essential for improving city life.

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:48.749
Those early days when we built the

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:50.699
movement behind the project, it was

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:51.869
around transit.

00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:53.789
The three COVID relief bills set

00:01:53.790 --> 00:01:55.619
aside $69 billion

00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:57.449
in federal funding for local transit

00:01:57.450 --> 00:01:59.399
agencies to operate and add

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:01.019
to their transportation systems,

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:03.149
meaning that Atlanta might finally

00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:05.249
get its train with many American

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:06.989
taxpayers who will never step foot

00:02:06.990 --> 00:02:08.999
on it, picking up much of the tab.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:10.619
Many American cities have used

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:12.509
federal money to build rail transit

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:14.279
lines that suffer from dismal

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:16.169
ridership, that are expensive to

00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:18.359
maintain, and that are a major

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:20.279
drain on their budgets.

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:21.659
Buses don't have the aesthetic

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:23.579
appeal of trains and don't evoke

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:24.580
the dense pre-automobile 19th-century

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:27.959
esthetic that many urbanists

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:29.189
romanticize.

00:02:29.190 --> 00:02:31.049
But they're far less costly and

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:31.919
more convenient.

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:34.379
And commuters generally prioritize

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:36.269
modes of transit that get them from

00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:38.309
point A to point B

00:02:38.310 --> 00:02:40.229
with the least amount of hassle.

00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:41.609
The sharp decline in the number of

00:02:41.610 --> 00:02:43.349
workers showing up at an office

00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:45.059
every day post-pandemic makes

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:47.219
building commuter rail an even worse

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:49.229
use of public funds, says Baruch

00:02:49.230 --> 00:02:51.659
Feigenbaum, senior managing director

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:53.748
of transportation policy at Reason

00:02:53.749 --> 00:02:54.809
Foundation, the nonprofit that

00:02:54.810 --> 00:02:56.279
publishes Reason TV and Reason

00:02:56.280 --> 00:02:57.149
magazine.

00:02:57.150 --> 00:02:58.439
You do need a public transit

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:00.239
network. It's absolutely true, but

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:01.979
it's the quality of the network and

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:03.539
not the actual mode that matters.

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:05.459
So there's been a major change

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:07.559
that we've really not seen

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:09.809
since pre-World War Two days.

00:03:09.810 --> 00:03:12.059
And that change is overwhelmingly

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:14.339
negative for transit, especially

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:16.289
rail transit, that we're seeing rail

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:18.659
ridership at about 40%,

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:20.609
maybe 50% of pre-pandemic

00:03:20.610 --> 00:03:22.529
levels, and bus ridership at

00:03:22.530 --> 00:03:24.119
60 to 70%.

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:25.739
And I think most people would tell

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:27.809
you if transit

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:30.389
ridership overall gets back to 80%

00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:31.859
of pre-pandemic levels, that would

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:33.329
be a wild success.

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:34.559
Most people do not think that's

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:35.609
going to happen.

00:03:35.610 --> 00:03:37.469
11 years ago, Feigenbaum wrote that

00:03:37.470 --> 00:03:39.209
the proposed Beltline train was

00:03:39.210 --> 00:03:41.489
possibly the worst transit project

00:03:41.490 --> 00:03:42.569
of all time.

00:03:42.570 --> 00:03:45.059
I think it's amazingly still true.

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:46.889
And the reason I say that is

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:48.899
because it's very

00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:50.939
expensive and it doesn't connect

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:52.859
people from point A to point B.

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:54.929
People don't go on transit

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:56.759
from one's house to another one's

00:03:56.760 --> 00:03:59.129
house. And that's basically the only

00:03:59.130 --> 00:04:01.559
places that this rail line served.

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:04.259
And so there was no clear ridership

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:05.489
for this train.

00:04:05.490 --> 00:04:07.319
So they came up with these kind

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:08.320
of East-West side lines

00:04:09.510 --> 00:04:11.879
that they said, well, you're right,

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:13.739
there's not any destinations on

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:15.149
this beltline, but we're going to

00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:16.708
build these other lines, and then

00:04:16.709 --> 00:04:18.749
people are going to take them.

00:04:18.750 --> 00:04:20.309
And so the two problems with that

00:04:20.310 --> 00:04:22.769
are, one, more expensive

00:04:22.770 --> 00:04:24.119
in order to build these lines and

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:25.559
they had not identified funding for

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:27.599
the first big circle, not to mention

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:28.379
these other.

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:30.419
Two, every time people

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:32.369
have to make a connection or have to

00:04:32.370 --> 00:04:34.769
change lines, you lose ridership.

00:04:34.770 --> 00:04:36.509
A lot of politicians like Rail

00:04:36.510 --> 00:04:38.189
because they say it's permanent and

00:04:38.190 --> 00:04:39.959
we can't change it.

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:42.029
And ok that's true,

00:04:42.030 --> 00:04:43.949
but sometimes you want to change it.

00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:45.689
Gravel says that the emergence of

00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:47.459
restaurants and the thriving Ponce

00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:48.959
City Market along the Beltline

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:50.789
demonstrates that when you build new

00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:53.099
infrastructure, commerce gravitates

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:53.789
to it.

00:04:53.790 --> 00:04:55.289
Ponce City Market is a major

00:04:55.290 --> 00:04:56.459
destination.

00:04:56.460 --> 00:04:57.839
All these buildings being built

00:04:57.840 --> 00:04:59.309
across the way: some of the best

00:04:59.310 --> 00:05:01.189
architecture in the city, some of

00:05:01.190 --> 00:05:02.249
the coolest places to build, some of

00:05:02.250 --> 00:05:04.299
the best companies to work for.

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:05.159
You know why this area?

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:06.160
Because of the Beltline. They're

00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:07.799
there for other reasons.

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:09.055
But also the Beltline.

00:05:09.056 --> 00:05:10.799
We're building a future city.

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:12.689
And look at it, proven by the

00:05:12.690 --> 00:05:14.759
last 22 years, it's getting built

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:15.899
in front of our eyes.

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:17.819
The best time to start transit is

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:18.869
ten years ago.

00:05:18.870 --> 00:05:20.669
The second best time is now.

00:05:20.670 --> 00:05:22.229
Matthew Roush is the chair of

00:05:22.230 --> 00:05:24.149
Beltline Rail Now a

00:05:24.150 --> 00:05:25.889
group of activists pushing for the

00:05:25.890 --> 00:05:27.809
completion of Gravel's vision.

00:05:27.810 --> 00:05:29.279
Because it's never getting less

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:30.119
expensive.

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:31.499
We're talking about one-time

00:05:31.500 --> 00:05:33.779
investments in the life of a city,

00:05:33.780 --> 00:05:35.279
and because they're more expensive,

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:37.259
means we need to go and figure out

00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:38.639
how to get more money.

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:40.229
But rail isn't really a one-time

00:05:40.230 --> 00:05:41.159
investment.

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:42.779
While Rao's coalition wants the

00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:44.219
federal government to foot much of

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:46.109
the bill for rail construction,

00:05:46.110 --> 00:05:47.669
local taxpayers are usually

00:05:47.670 --> 00:05:49.259
responsible for the majority of

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:51.149
operating costs for all forms

00:05:51.150 --> 00:05:51.989
of transit.

00:05:51.990 --> 00:05:53.819
In 2020, transit fares

00:05:53.820 --> 00:05:55.439
for light rail and streetcars

00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:57.569
covered about 14%

00:05:57.570 --> 00:05:59.579
of operating costs nationwide.

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:01.079
Getting federal dollars to build

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:02.849
rail is kind of like being gifted a

00:06:02.850 --> 00:06:04.649
swimming pool that you barely use

00:06:04.650 --> 00:06:06.239
and then being left to cover the

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:07.619
maintenance costs.

00:06:07.620 --> 00:06:08.849
It might have sounded like a good

00:06:08.850 --> 00:06:09.719
idea.

00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:11.309
There is some local enthusiasm for

00:06:11.310 --> 00:06:12.269
the rail project.

00:06:12.270 --> 00:06:13.889
Atlanta voters approved a half-cent

00:06:13.890 --> 00:06:15.479
sales tax in 2016.

00:06:15.480 --> 00:06:17.309
That, in part, was supposed to

00:06:17.310 --> 00:06:18.299
fund the construction of the

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:19.199
Beltline train.

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:20.789
But the city's public transit

00:06:20.790 --> 00:06:22.739
agency, known as MARTA, has been

00:06:22.740 --> 00:06:24.809
using that funding instead

00:06:24.810 --> 00:06:27.149
to improve current operations,

00:06:27.150 --> 00:06:28.649
especially bus service.

00:06:28.650 --> 00:06:30.449
The agency's most recent analysis

00:06:30.450 --> 00:06:31.679
projected that it would cost an

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:33.529
estimated 287 million

00:06:33.530 --> 00:06:35.789
to $448 million

00:06:35.790 --> 00:06:37.169
to build just the first three miles

00:06:37.170 --> 00:06:38.249
of track.

00:06:38.250 --> 00:06:40.649
That's up from $172 million,

00:06:40.650 --> 00:06:42.119
which was the estimated cost just

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:43.019
three years ago.

00:06:43.020 --> 00:06:44.189
We're here in L.A.

00:06:44.190 --> 00:06:46.049
to look at bus rapid transit

00:06:46.050 --> 00:06:47.699
to see how we can transfer the best

00:06:47.700 --> 00:06:49.919
practices back to Atlanta.

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:51.149
MARTA wants Atlanta to emulate the

00:06:51.150 --> 00:06:52.769
bus service in cities like Los

00:06:52.770 --> 00:06:54.449
Angeles that have dedicated bus

00:06:54.450 --> 00:06:56.429
lanes and synchronized stoplights to

00:06:56.430 --> 00:06:58.049
minimize traffic.

00:06:58.050 --> 00:07:00.119
Bus lanes cost far less to maintain

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:01.709
than rail, and their routes are

00:07:01.710 --> 00:07:04.319
modified if demand rises for false.

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:05.489
Several studies have found that

00:07:05.490 --> 00:07:07.379
people who can't afford cars rely

00:07:07.380 --> 00:07:09.119
on buses more than trains.

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:10.889
And MARTA recently abandoned a light

00:07:10.890 --> 00:07:12.929
rail project in favor of bus

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:14.789
rapid transit, saying it would

00:07:14.790 --> 00:07:16.619
provide greater mobility for far

00:07:16.620 --> 00:07:17.639
less money.

00:07:17.640 --> 00:07:19.559
People rely on what they have, and

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:20.669
that's what we have.

00:07:20.670 --> 00:07:22.469
There's nothing wrong with buses.

00:07:22.470 --> 00:07:24.329
Buses are the core of

00:07:24.330 --> 00:07:26.339
any city's mass transit

00:07:26.340 --> 00:07:27.839
system. What we're talking about

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:30.419
here is a different kind of project

00:07:30.420 --> 00:07:32.309
that is higher in capacity

00:07:32.310 --> 00:07:34.139
and creates also

00:07:34.140 --> 00:07:35.999
a beautiful place for everyone in

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:37.709
the city to access.

00:07:37.710 --> 00:07:39.359
Atlanta residents don't have to look

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:41.279
far for an example of a streetcar

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:42.779
like the one the coalition wants to

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:44.279
build on the Beltline.

00:07:44.280 --> 00:07:46.049
They already have one that stretches

00:07:46.050 --> 00:07:48.269
2.7 miles through the downtown

00:07:48.270 --> 00:07:50.159
area, and this is what it

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:52.109
looks like on a weekday afternoon

00:07:52.110 --> 00:07:53.969
at about 3 p.m.

00:07:53.970 --> 00:07:55.559
its average speed is about five

00:07:55.560 --> 00:07:57.599
miles per hour, about the same speed

00:07:57.600 --> 00:07:59.099
as the horse-drawn railways that

00:07:59.100 --> 00:08:00.479
crisscross cities, including

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:02.189
Atlanta, in the 19th century.

00:08:02.190 --> 00:08:03.689
The streetcar stops every quarter

00:08:03.690 --> 00:08:05.639
mile. Less than a thousand people

00:08:05.640 --> 00:08:07.499
ride the streetcar daily in a

00:08:07.500 --> 00:08:09.929
city 500 times that size.

00:08:09.930 --> 00:08:12.149
It cost more than $52 million

00:08:12.150 --> 00:08:14.489
per mile to build in 2013.

00:08:14.490 --> 00:08:16.469
The ridership on that is pretty

00:08:16.470 --> 00:08:17.039
low.

00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:18.309
So it's dismal.

00:08:18.310 --> 00:08:20.129
Yes. So what?

00:08:20.130 --> 00:08:21.419
What makes you think that this is

00:08:21.420 --> 00:08:22.559
going to be different?

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:24.569
I think the success of

00:08:24.570 --> 00:08:26.399
any transit project

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:28.229
is taking people from where they

00:08:28.230 --> 00:08:30.209
are to where they want to go or

00:08:30.210 --> 00:08:31.979
need to go. We'll be the first city

00:08:31.980 --> 00:08:34.139
and only city in the world to have

00:08:34.140 --> 00:08:35.009
this.

00:08:35.010 --> 00:08:37.109
This multi-purpose trail,

00:08:37.110 --> 00:08:39.149
this greenway, and

00:08:39.150 --> 00:08:40.769
the transit within it, and

00:08:40.770 --> 00:08:42.569
connecting to our existing heavy

00:08:42.570 --> 00:08:44.129
rail system.

00:08:44.130 --> 00:08:46.349
Even if ground broke tomorrow.

00:08:46.350 --> 00:08:48.539
MARTA estimates it could take five

00:08:48.540 --> 00:08:50.399
years to complete just the first

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:51.839
mile and a half of track.

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:53.309
There's a good chance that transit

00:08:53.310 --> 00:08:55.439
ridership will never fully come back

00:08:55.440 --> 00:08:57.599
post-pandemic because so many fewer

00:08:57.600 --> 00:08:59.969
people are commuting to jobs.

00:08:59.970 --> 00:09:01.889
Even in New York City, paid bus

00:09:01.890 --> 00:09:04.709
and subway ridership is at 55%

00:09:04.710 --> 00:09:06.479
of its pre-pandemic levels

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:07.349
nationwide.

00:09:07.350 --> 00:09:10.409
Transit ridership is at about 69%

00:09:10.410 --> 00:09:12.269
of what it was before COVID.

00:09:12.270 --> 00:09:14.339
Rail transit nationwide has taken

00:09:14.340 --> 00:09:16.349
a particularly bad hit,

00:09:16.350 --> 00:09:18.209
with ridership decreasing by almost

00:09:18.210 --> 00:09:20.069
50% between 2011 and

00:09:20.070 --> 00:09:21.929
2020, while nonrail

00:09:21.930 --> 00:09:24.659
transit decreased by about 30%.

00:09:24.660 --> 00:09:26.549
That trend applies in Atlanta,

00:09:26.550 --> 00:09:28.169
where MARTA's bus ridership

00:09:28.170 --> 00:09:30.449
decreased by 39%

00:09:30.450 --> 00:09:32.759
post-COVID, while rail decreased

00:09:32.760 --> 00:09:34.949
by about 62%.

00:09:34.950 --> 00:09:37.259
Before COVID, only 5%

00:09:37.260 --> 00:09:38.579
of Americans' working days were

00:09:38.580 --> 00:09:39.869
spent at home.

00:09:39.870 --> 00:09:41.789
Researchers at Stanford analyzed

00:09:41.790 --> 00:09:43.829
poll data and concluded that now

00:09:43.830 --> 00:09:45.539
about half of Americans work at home

00:09:45.540 --> 00:09:47.369
at least once a week, and about a

00:09:47.370 --> 00:09:49.499
quarter are fully remote.

00:09:49.500 --> 00:09:51.089
The pandemic also accelerated

00:09:51.090 --> 00:09:52.769
the decade-long trend of Americans

00:09:52.770 --> 00:09:54.959
moving from cities to lower-density

00:09:54.960 --> 00:09:56.999
suburbs, including the area

00:09:57.000 --> 00:09:58.679
surrounding the city of Atlanta,

00:09:58.680 --> 00:10:00.589
which saw population loss in its

00:10:00.590 --> 00:10:02.449
urban core for the first time

00:10:02.450 --> 00:10:04.639
in a decade between 2020

00:10:04.640 --> 00:10:06.769
and 2021, Rao says

00:10:06.770 --> 00:10:07.999
it's just a blip.

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:09.979
This corridor, which is half a mile

00:10:09.980 --> 00:10:11.869
wide, will contain between

00:10:11.870 --> 00:10:13.789
23 and 25% of

00:10:13.790 --> 00:10:16.069
the population of this whole city

00:10:16.070 --> 00:10:17.629
by mid-century.

00:10:17.630 --> 00:10:19.459
And that kind of density will

00:10:19.460 --> 00:10:21.559
only work sustainably

00:10:21.560 --> 00:10:24.409
if we provide the transit.

00:10:24.410 --> 00:10:26.209
Downtown Atlanta is booming.

00:10:26.210 --> 00:10:27.919
You have more freedom if you can

00:10:27.920 --> 00:10:29.449
telework so people, they go where

00:10:29.450 --> 00:10:30.529
they want to go.

00:10:30.530 --> 00:10:31.819
I know that's surprising for a lot

00:10:31.820 --> 00:10:33.109
of people, but people do want to be

00:10:33.110 --> 00:10:34.519
downtown. They want to be on the

00:10:34.520 --> 00:10:36.019
Beltline. They want to be here.

00:10:36.020 --> 00:10:37.399
There's so much to do.

00:10:37.400 --> 00:10:38.239
You can walk.

00:10:38.240 --> 00:10:39.679
You can ride scooters.

00:10:39.680 --> 00:10:40.849
You can ride your bike.

00:10:40.850 --> 00:10:42.949
I walk the Beltline practically

00:10:42.950 --> 00:10:43.879
four times a week.

00:10:43.880 --> 00:10:45.349
It's pretty integral to my life.

00:10:45.350 --> 00:10:48.109
I like the skateboard and

00:10:48.110 --> 00:10:50.449
just have fun and be drawn.

00:10:50.450 --> 00:10:52.639
While Ponce City Market is a popular

00:10:52.640 --> 00:10:54.469
destination, much of the loop is

00:10:54.470 --> 00:10:56.329
still a nature trail with nothing

00:10:56.330 --> 00:10:58.549
but single-family homes around it.

00:10:58.550 --> 00:11:00.319
Gravel's master's thesis opens with

00:11:00.320 --> 00:11:02.659
a history of Atlanta transportation,

00:11:02.660 --> 00:11:04.099
pointing out that streetcars were

00:11:04.100 --> 00:11:05.839
profitable in many American cities

00:11:05.840 --> 00:11:07.519
before they were displaced by roads

00:11:07.520 --> 00:11:09.049
and cars.

00:11:09.050 --> 00:11:11.089
Is that something that you think

00:11:11.090 --> 00:11:13.129
we can ever get back to, where

00:11:13.130 --> 00:11:15.019
there could be an actual, you know,

00:11:15.020 --> 00:11:17.299
profitable streetcar?

00:11:17.300 --> 00:11:19.459
It's a good question.

00:11:19.460 --> 00:11:21.379
From my personal viewpoint,

00:11:22.940 --> 00:11:24.949
I think I think there's a shared

00:11:24.950 --> 00:11:26.389
good that comes with public

00:11:26.390 --> 00:11:27.499
investment and transit.

00:11:27.500 --> 00:11:28.759
And so I'm fully supportive of

00:11:28.760 --> 00:11:29.719
public transit.

00:11:29.720 --> 00:11:31.639
But like the military,

00:11:31.640 --> 00:11:33.199
like highways, like all kinds of

00:11:33.200 --> 00:11:34.519
things, there are there are things

00:11:34.520 --> 00:11:35.809
that are good for all of us that we

00:11:35.810 --> 00:11:38.269
should be investing in collectively.

00:11:38.270 --> 00:11:39.769
This wasn't always the case.

00:11:39.770 --> 00:11:41.599
In the 19th century, urban transit

00:11:41.600 --> 00:11:43.159
systems were privately owned and

00:11:43.160 --> 00:11:44.089
operated.

00:11:44.090 --> 00:11:45.859
Companies would sometimes lose money

00:11:45.860 --> 00:11:47.179
at the fare box to encourage

00:11:47.180 --> 00:11:49.189
ridership but make it back by

00:11:49.190 --> 00:11:51.139
speculating in land that increased

00:11:51.140 --> 00:11:52.609
in value because of the new train

00:11:52.610 --> 00:11:53.449
station.

00:11:53.450 --> 00:11:55.129
When local governments did build

00:11:55.130 --> 00:11:56.539
infrastructure, they would sometimes

00:11:56.540 --> 00:11:58.339
pay for it by levying special

00:11:58.340 --> 00:12:00.049
assessments on land owners who

00:12:00.050 --> 00:12:00.889
benefited.

00:12:00.890 --> 00:12:02.809
Rouse Coalition is proposing just

00:12:02.810 --> 00:12:04.069
such a special levy along the

00:12:04.070 --> 00:12:05.749
Beltline, but it would cover just

00:12:05.750 --> 00:12:07.919
10% of the projected $2.5

00:12:07.920 --> 00:12:10.039
billion construction cost.

00:12:10.040 --> 00:12:12.289
Federal taxpayers would foot 40%

00:12:12.290 --> 00:12:13.489
of the bill. If the local

00:12:13.490 --> 00:12:15.109
stakeholders who stand to benefit

00:12:15.110 --> 00:12:16.969
from increased property values had

00:12:16.970 --> 00:12:18.469
to foot more of the bill.

00:12:18.470 --> 00:12:19.999
Maybe they would think twice.

00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:21.619
While rail and city buses

00:12:21.620 --> 00:12:23.389
consistently lose money.

00:12:23.390 --> 00:12:25.369
Buses that move people between

00:12:25.370 --> 00:12:27.289
cities are still privately owned

00:12:27.290 --> 00:12:28.999
and operated at a profit.

00:12:29.000 --> 00:12:30.949
Amtrak, on the other hand, loses

00:12:30.950 --> 00:12:32.779
close to $1,000,000,000 annually

00:12:32.780 --> 00:12:34.759
and relies on federal subsidies.

00:12:34.760 --> 00:12:36.589
The bill I'm about to sign

00:12:36.590 --> 00:12:38.599
a lot is proof that despite

00:12:38.600 --> 00:12:40.399
the cynics, Democrats and

00:12:40.400 --> 00:12:42.379
Republicans can

00:12:42.380 --> 00:12:44.659
come together and deliver results.

00:12:44.660 --> 00:12:45.859
It makes the most significant

00:12:45.860 --> 00:12:47.839
investment in passenger rail

00:12:47.840 --> 00:12:49.679
in the past 50 years

00:12:49.680 --> 00:12:51.919
in public transit ever.

00:12:51.920 --> 00:12:53.629
Feigenbaum says many politicians

00:12:53.630 --> 00:12:55.129
seem to care less about whether

00:12:55.130 --> 00:12:56.629
transportation projects are best

00:12:56.630 --> 00:12:58.489
serving their constituents

00:12:58.490 --> 00:13:00.589
and much more about the spectacle.

00:13:00.590 --> 00:13:02.419
If I'm a politician and

00:13:02.420 --> 00:13:04.279
I'm running for reelection, I

00:13:04.280 --> 00:13:05.509
get to go to a ribbon-cutting

00:13:05.510 --> 00:13:07.789
ceremony and say, I did this

00:13:07.790 --> 00:13:09.559
and I get to send out a postcard to

00:13:09.560 --> 00:13:11.329
all of the voters in my district and

00:13:11.330 --> 00:13:12.349
say, I did this.

00:13:12.350 --> 00:13:14.509
I'm here working for you, using

00:13:14.510 --> 00:13:17.089
your taxpayer money in dubious ways.

00:13:17.090 --> 00:13:19.099
Whereas if you're

00:13:19.100 --> 00:13:21.049
talking about operations so there's

00:13:21.050 --> 00:13:22.849
no ribbon cuttings with operations,

00:13:22.850 --> 00:13:24.829
it's not sexy, it's not something

00:13:24.830 --> 00:13:26.659
you can sell saying, Oh,

00:13:26.660 --> 00:13:28.849
your bus is coming every 5 minutes

00:13:28.850 --> 00:13:30.739
as opposed to every 8 minutes gets

00:13:30.740 --> 00:13:31.879
a real yawn.

00:13:31.880 --> 00:13:34.039
In a political type environment.

00:13:34.040 --> 00:13:36.019
Atlanta's rail advocates say that

00:13:36.020 --> 00:13:37.549
MARTA's decision to allocate the

00:13:37.550 --> 00:13:39.709
money from the tax levy on improving

00:13:39.710 --> 00:13:41.539
bus operations defies the

00:13:41.540 --> 00:13:42.829
will of voters.

00:13:42.830 --> 00:13:44.749
What we're about is making sure

00:13:44.750 --> 00:13:46.279
there's transparency and

00:13:46.280 --> 00:13:48.409
accountability and what our public

00:13:48.410 --> 00:13:50.539
officials and public institutions

00:13:50.540 --> 00:13:52.249
are doing with the money that we're

00:13:52.250 --> 00:13:54.079
giving them to achieve

00:13:54.080 --> 00:13:55.129
an outcome.

00:13:55.130 --> 00:13:57.169
And if there isn't enough money to

00:13:57.170 --> 00:13:59.149
build the projects

00:13:59.150 --> 00:14:00.739
that MARTA said it could build for

00:14:00.740 --> 00:14:02.629
the money we said we would pay

00:14:02.630 --> 00:14:04.609
then I think it's up to agencies

00:14:04.610 --> 00:14:06.259
like MARTA and our leaders at the

00:14:06.260 --> 00:14:08.149
city to tell us that and

00:14:08.150 --> 00:14:10.009
to give us the opportunity as

00:14:10.010 --> 00:14:11.329
to how to react to that.

00:14:11.330 --> 00:14:13.339
There was a package of things.

00:14:13.340 --> 00:14:15.109
The Beltline was one of the things

00:14:15.110 --> 00:14:16.879
in the package, and there were other

00:14:16.880 --> 00:14:19.009
things in that package, including

00:14:19.010 --> 00:14:20.839
increased bus service

00:14:20.840 --> 00:14:22.669
and making sure that there was some

00:14:22.670 --> 00:14:24.979
better technology on the rail lines.

00:14:24.980 --> 00:14:26.839
We really don't know, and I

00:14:26.840 --> 00:14:28.729
don't think they know why

00:14:28.730 --> 00:14:29.839
this passed.

00:14:29.840 --> 00:14:31.549
And I should also note that there

00:14:31.550 --> 00:14:33.529
has to be some ability

00:14:33.530 --> 00:14:35.419
for a transit agency to make

00:14:35.420 --> 00:14:37.909
some types of minor changes when

00:14:37.910 --> 00:14:39.829
a major world-changing event

00:14:39.830 --> 00:14:41.409
like COVID-19 happens.

00:14:41.410 --> 00:14:43.069
You know, MARTA didn't know

00:14:43.070 --> 00:14:44.749
that COVID-19 was going to happen,

00:14:44.750 --> 00:14:45.949
and they certainly could not have

00:14:45.950 --> 00:14:47.929
projected what changes were going

00:14:47.930 --> 00:14:50.239
to happen as a result of that.

00:14:50.240 --> 00:14:52.099
The two COVID relief bills, one

00:14:52.100 --> 00:14:53.959
signed by Trump, the other by Biden,

00:14:53.960 --> 00:14:55.819
sent $69 billion in

00:14:55.820 --> 00:14:57.199
additional funding to America's

00:14:57.200 --> 00:14:59.089
transit agencies at a time.

00:14:59.090 --> 00:15:00.379
When ridership is falling.

00:15:00.380 --> 00:15:02.269
This country for basically as

00:15:02.270 --> 00:15:03.679
long as you and I have been alive

00:15:03.680 --> 00:15:05.539
has been under-investing in

00:15:05.540 --> 00:15:06.322
rail.

00:15:06.323 --> 00:15:07.579
Pete Buttigieg, is, of course,

00:15:07.580 --> 00:15:08.689
running for president.

00:15:08.690 --> 00:15:10.249
And so he's going to say things that

00:15:10.250 --> 00:15:11.419
a politician would.

00:15:11.420 --> 00:15:13.189
If you spend more money on rail

00:15:13.190 --> 00:15:14.509
projects where they're not needed,

00:15:14.510 --> 00:15:16.279
which is most places in the country,

00:15:16.280 --> 00:15:17.209
you're going to be wasting your

00:15:17.210 --> 00:15:19.249
money. I think if Pete Buttigieg

00:15:19.250 --> 00:15:21.649
would go down a couple of floors

00:15:21.650 --> 00:15:23.419
in the DOT building and talk to his

00:15:23.420 --> 00:15:25.609
transit planners and his folks

00:15:25.610 --> 00:15:27.269
who have been there for more than 5

00:15:27.270 --> 00:15:29.239
minutes, he would realize that

00:15:29.240 --> 00:15:30.739
this doesn't necessarily make a lot

00:15:30.740 --> 00:15:32.149
of sense. It means you're going to

00:15:32.150 --> 00:15:34.009
have fewer bus lines that you

00:15:34.010 --> 00:15:36.049
need because spending money on rail

00:15:36.050 --> 00:15:37.279
is inherently going to take money

00:15:37.280 --> 00:15:38.239
from bus.

00:15:38.240 --> 00:15:39.589
It means you're going to have fewer

00:15:39.590 --> 00:15:41.389
transportation resources that you

00:15:41.390 --> 00:15:43.369
need, probably roadways, but

00:15:43.370 --> 00:15:44.839
could be other things such as bike

00:15:44.840 --> 00:15:45.929
lanes and sidewalks.

00:15:45.930 --> 00:15:47.719
You're going to be spending funds

00:15:47.720 --> 00:15:49.549
that you just don't

00:15:49.550 --> 00:15:51.559
need in an economy that is already

00:15:51.560 --> 00:15:53.029
overheated.

00:15:53.030 --> 00:15:54.559
Buttigieg has been a critic of how

00:15:54.560 --> 00:15:56.209
cars reshaped the national

00:15:56.210 --> 00:15:57.109
landscape.

00:15:57.110 --> 00:15:58.789
He said that the interstate highway

00:15:58.790 --> 00:16:00.769
system is systemically racist

00:16:00.770 --> 00:16:02.119
because it cut through historic

00:16:02.120 --> 00:16:03.979
black and immigrant neighborhoods.

00:16:03.980 --> 00:16:05.809
If a highway was

00:16:05.810 --> 00:16:08.059
built for the purpose of dividing

00:16:08.060 --> 00:16:09.889
a white and a black neighborhood,

00:16:09.890 --> 00:16:11.899
or if an underpass was

00:16:11.900 --> 00:16:13.759
constructed such that a bus

00:16:13.760 --> 00:16:15.079
carrying mostly black and Puerto

00:16:15.080 --> 00:16:17.089
Rican kids to a beach

00:16:17.090 --> 00:16:18.829
or that would have been in New York

00:16:18.830 --> 00:16:20.689
was was designed too low for it to

00:16:20.690 --> 00:16:22.729
pass by. But that obviously reflects

00:16:22.730 --> 00:16:24.349
racism that went into those design

00:16:24.350 --> 00:16:25.069
choices.

00:16:25.070 --> 00:16:26.989
You can question whether we

00:16:26.990 --> 00:16:28.399
should have built the highway system

00:16:28.400 --> 00:16:30.229
in the exact form that we did.

00:16:30.230 --> 00:16:31.219
I think those are all fair

00:16:31.220 --> 00:16:32.749
questions. But this idea that

00:16:32.750 --> 00:16:34.219
somehow we spent a lot of money on

00:16:34.220 --> 00:16:36.019
highways and so now it's time to

00:16:36.020 --> 00:16:37.759
spend a lot of money on railways

00:16:37.760 --> 00:16:39.679
because that's just the right thing

00:16:39.680 --> 00:16:41.059
to do or that's just going to solve

00:16:41.060 --> 00:16:42.709
our problems, magically, doesn't

00:16:42.710 --> 00:16:43.789
pencil out.

00:16:43.790 --> 00:16:45.649
Buses, replace rail, not

00:16:45.650 --> 00:16:48.049
because of conspiracy, but because

00:16:48.050 --> 00:16:49.279
they're much more flexible

00:16:49.280 --> 00:16:50.359
and cost-effective.

00:16:50.360 --> 00:16:51.769
Transit should cater to what

00:16:51.770 --> 00:16:53.689
customers want, which is fast

00:16:53.690 --> 00:16:54.690
and
convenient, point-to-point mobility.

00:16:56.690 --> 00:16:58.669
Reimagining what transit

00:16:58.670 --> 00:17:00.709
is, which is it's not just

00:17:00.710 --> 00:17:02.929
an agency, it's services

00:17:02.930 --> 00:17:04.879
that are provided to

00:17:04.880 --> 00:17:06.529
your customers, which are people who

00:17:06.530 --> 00:17:07.729
are paying for it.

00:17:07.730 --> 00:17:09.769
And so we're not just talking about

00:17:09.770 --> 00:17:11.269
fixed rail and bus.

00:17:11.270 --> 00:17:13.098
We're talking about the Ubers

00:17:13.099 --> 00:17:13.939
and Lifts.

00:17:13.940 --> 00:17:15.348
We're talking about on-demand

00:17:15.349 --> 00:17:16.699
transit. We're talking

00:17:16.700 --> 00:17:18.469
about micro-transit, which is

00:17:18.470 --> 00:17:20.539
transit that isn't travel on a fixed

00:17:20.540 --> 00:17:22.459
route. It might go based on where

00:17:22.460 --> 00:17:23.959
it's needed. We're talking about

00:17:23.960 --> 00:17:24.799
vanpool.

00:17:24.800 --> 00:17:25.789
We're talking about sort of a

00:17:25.790 --> 00:17:27.649
concept of mobility

00:17:27.650 --> 00:17:29.389
that might not, in some cases,

00:17:29.390 --> 00:17:31.279
involve people owning cars, which

00:17:31.280 --> 00:17:33.109
is okay if they don't want to

00:17:33.110 --> 00:17:33.889
own a car.

00:17:33.890 --> 00:17:35.299
And we have a system that works for

00:17:35.300 --> 00:17:37.189
that. But I think that type

00:17:37.190 --> 00:17:39.049
of approach is going to be

00:17:39.050 --> 00:17:41.419
much better than forcing people

00:17:41.420 --> 00:17:43.339
to give up cars because we make

00:17:43.340 --> 00:17:44.930
it so difficult to drive.

00:17:46.730 --> 00:17:48.649
Paris's walkability is what

00:17:48.650 --> 00:17:50.809
first inspired Ryan Gravel

00:17:50.810 --> 00:17:52.969
to re-imagine the Atlanta cityscape

00:17:52.970 --> 00:17:54.529
in the late nineties.

00:17:54.530 --> 00:17:56.569
Today, Feigenbaum suggests looking

00:17:56.570 --> 00:17:58.339
to France for a different lesson

00:17:58.340 --> 00:17:59.479
about mobility.

00:17:59.480 --> 00:18:01.249
France, which is not exactly known

00:18:01.250 --> 00:18:03.349
for being a free market capitalist

00:18:03.350 --> 00:18:04.159
capital.

00:18:04.160 --> 00:18:06.049
The private sector operates all of

00:18:06.050 --> 00:18:07.339
their transit services, and it's

00:18:07.340 --> 00:18:08.359
much better.

00:18:08.360 --> 00:18:09.859
You'd also need some type of

00:18:09.860 --> 00:18:11.689
vouchers to make sure that

00:18:11.690 --> 00:18:13.309
the lower-income folks who are the

00:18:13.310 --> 00:18:15.019
most dependent on transit can

00:18:15.020 --> 00:18:16.609
actually access that and can reach

00:18:16.610 --> 00:18:18.019
the jobs in other places that they

00:18:18.020 --> 00:18:18.949
need to go.

00:18:18.950 --> 00:18:20.779
But I think this is the time we need

00:18:20.780 --> 00:18:22.609
to start asking those questions,

00:18:22.610 --> 00:18:24.499
because in the wake of COVID, we're

00:18:24.500 --> 00:18:25.669
not going to get back to the

00:18:25.670 --> 00:18:27.499
ridership we had in 2019,

00:18:27.500 --> 00:18:29.179
and the ridership we had in 2019

00:18:29.180 --> 00:18:30.439
wasn't that good anyway?

00:18:30.440 --> 00:18:32.329
Is there a sense in which,

00:18:32.330 --> 00:18:34.069
you know, the American city is just

00:18:34.070 --> 00:18:36.109
different from a city like

00:18:36.110 --> 00:18:38.539
Paris and or can't

00:18:38.540 --> 00:18:40.309
graph that onto a place like

00:18:40.310 --> 00:18:41.089
Atlanta?

00:18:41.090 --> 00:18:43.129
Do you worry that you are

00:18:43.130 --> 00:18:45.919
kind of fighting an inevitable

00:18:45.920 --> 00:18:47.539
growth pattern?

00:18:47.540 --> 00:18:48.199
No.

00:18:48.200 --> 00:18:49.159
No.

00:18:49.160 --> 00:18:50.719
Not at all.

00:18:50.720 --> 00:18:52.089
I'm not worried about that at all.

00:18:52.090 --> 00:18:53.107
You know, Atlanta is not Paris,

00:18:53.108 --> 00:18:54.949
Atlanta is not New York, Atlanta is

00:18:54.950 --> 00:18:55.969
not Houston.

00:18:55.970 --> 00:18:58.489
You know, like Atlanta is Atlanta.

00:18:58.490 --> 00:19:00.169
And Atlanta needs to grow in the way

00:19:00.170 --> 00:19:01.489
that it grows into the best version

00:19:01.490 --> 00:19:02.569
of itself.

00:19:02.570 --> 00:19:04.459
Atlanta's history is railroads.

00:19:04.460 --> 00:19:06.919
Its future is also railroads.

00:19:06.920 --> 00:19:07.999
MARTA still hasn't committed to

00:19:08.000 --> 00:19:09.619
building rail on the Beltline and

00:19:09.620 --> 00:19:11.599
says the issue still needs two

00:19:11.600 --> 00:19:13.249
more years of study before the board

00:19:13.250 --> 00:19:14.119
can vote on it.

00:19:14.120 --> 00:19:16.309
The rail advocates we spoke to worry

00:19:16.310 --> 00:19:18.199
that by then it will be too late

00:19:18.200 --> 00:19:19.819
to qualify for federal funding for a

00:19:19.820 --> 00:19:21.679
project that's taken more than 20

00:19:21.680 --> 00:19:23.119
years already.

00:19:23.120 --> 00:19:24.049
Gravel is right.

00:19:24.050 --> 00:19:25.309
The rail lines were good for

00:19:25.310 --> 00:19:27.019
American cities because they were

00:19:27.020 --> 00:19:29.269
heavily used and often profitable.

00:19:29.270 --> 00:19:31.339
So what are the chances

00:19:31.340 --> 00:19:33.589
that today a project expected

00:19:33.590 --> 00:19:35.479
from the outset to operate at a

00:19:35.480 --> 00:19:37.219
loss during a period of declining

00:19:37.220 --> 00:19:39.289
rail transit use and that depends

00:19:39.290 --> 00:19:41.209
on over $100 million

00:19:41.210 --> 00:19:43.189
in federal subsidies is

00:19:43.190 --> 00:19:45.109
going to be something that Atlanta

00:19:45.110 --> 00:19:46.519
commuters actually use?

